{"preference":"B","margin":5,"reasons":"The task is to revise without changing what the summary says. B keeps the design-supported claim: a non-randomized internal pilot comparison, an 11 vs 8 point difference, and an evaluator who explicitly says the results do not yet show causation. It states the selection-bias threat and says what would support a causal claim (the 2026-27 randomized study). It also tells the board how to describe the results (preliminary), which is what makes it actionable. A converts a non-randomized comparison into proof ('proves the model works', 'The effect was unmistakable', 'the answer is in'). It reverses the evaluator's hedge in the quotation ('encouraging but do not yet show that tutoring caused the gains' becomes 'encouraging and show that tutoring works'). It presents the randomized study as a formality that will 'confirm' a conclusion already drawn. It also reports '38 percent advantage' (3/8 = 37.5 percent) as a relative gain on a scale-score difference with no uncertainty, which overstates a 3-point raw difference. A also uses 'nearly 5,000' for 4,218 students, which inflates reach and changes the per-student figure's apparent basis, and drops the session length. A misrepresents the evidence and the evaluator, so it would mislead a budget vote.","objections":[{"candidate":"A","category":"causal_overreach","quote":"a 38 percent advantage that proves the model works","claim":"A non-randomized comparison of gain scores cannot prove causation; selection into tutoring is an uncontrolled confound. 'Proves' is unsupported by the design.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"A","category":"altered_quotation","quote":"\"the results are encouraging and show that tutoring works.\"","claim":"The evaluator's hedge ('but do not yet show that tutoring caused the gains') was removed and the sense reversed, which attributes to the evaluator a conclusion the evaluator did not draw.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"A","category":"pilot_as_finding","quote":"A randomized study planned for 2026-27 will confirm what the first year has already made clear.","claim":"Presents a future test as a formality and pre-judges its outcome. A randomized study could fail to replicate the effect.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"A","category":"missing_design_disclosure","quote":"The effect was unmistakable.","claim":"Omits that the comparison is an internal, non-randomized pilot, and gives no uncertainty or effect-size information for a 3-point difference.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"A","category":"sample_misstatement","quote":"nearly 5,000 students in grades 1 through 3","claim":"Rounds 4,218 up to nearly 5,000, inflating reach. Also drops the 30-minute session length.","severity":"minor"},{"candidate":"A","category":"relative_vs_absolute","quote":"a 38 percent advantage","claim":"Expresses a 3-point raw difference as a relative percentage, which makes it look larger. Also, a gap in gain scores is not a percentage advantage in reading skill.","severity":"minor"},{"candidate":"B","category":"uncertainty","quote":"so the difference may reflect which students were selected for tutoring rather than the tutoring itself.","claim":"Sound hedge, but it names selection only; it does not give the size of the sample, the comparison group's size, or any uncertainty interval, so the board cannot judge whether the 3 points is meaningful.","severity":"minor"},{"candidate":"B","category":"audience_fit","quote":"the district should describe these results as preliminary.","claim":"Gives a framing instruction but not a recommended action for the budget vote (for example, fund conditionally). It is still clearer than A.","severity":"minor"}],"allegations":[{"candidate":"A","quote":"a 38 percent advantage","claim":"The summary's own figures give a 3-point difference on a base of 8, which is 37.5 percent. This is a relative-gain figure, not a measured effect size."},{"candidate":"A","quote":"nearly 5,000 students","claim":"B reports 4,218 tutored students. If 4,218 is correct, 'nearly 5,000' overstates it."}],"out_of_lane_notes":"Both reproduce the $688 per student figure; $2.9M divided by 4,218 is about $688, so it is consistent with B's count and not with a count near 5,000. A's headline ('changed the trajectory') is also unsupported by a single year of data. B's structure (short version first, labelled sections) suits a one-read board audience."}